  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 1231 AM MDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nModels and ensembles are in good agreement through the weekend,\nwith difference arising by early to mid next week. Conditions\nwill gradually dry out by the weekend and heat up.\n\nThursday and Friday...the upper high will sit across the Four\nCorners region Thursday, and begin gradually migrating westward\non Friday. Expect another two days of showers and thunderstorms,\ninitiating across the higher terrain, and tracking southeast\nacross the Plains. PWAT values remain elevated, and\nsoutheasterly flow will keep dewpoints elevated across the\nPlains. Shear remain weak during this period, which will help\nkeep thunderstorms sub-severe, however, a few may produce small\nhail and strong winds. The main concern will once again be\nprecipitation efficiency. Flash flooding will remain possible\nboth days, especially on the Aspen Acres burn scar. Do not let\nyour guard down if you are in or near the burn scar through\nFriday! Temperatures will be hot on Thursday with 90s to a few\nlower 100s on the Plains, while the San Luis Valley sees 80s. A\ncold front arrives on Friday, with a few degrees of cooling, but\nwill still be hot. Showers and thunderstorms will spread east\ninto the Plains during the evening and overnight hours, clearing\nthe state by the early morning hours.\n\nSaturday into Monday...the upper ridge continues ot retrograde\nwest over the weekend, while and upper trough moves across the\nNorthern Rockies into the Northern Plains. PWAT values really\ndry out over the weekend, to near 0.5 to 0.8 inches. This will\nlikely limit thunderstorm coverage over the weekend into Monday.\nTemperatures will also warm during this period, with 90s across\nthe Plains Saturday, with widespread lower 100s Sunday into\nMonday. While it will be drier, isolated to scattered convection\nacross the higher terrain will still be possible. Lightning will\nbe of concern along with gusty outflow winds. With PWAT values\ncoming down, precipitation efficiency also diminishes. Burn \nscars will need to be monitored. \n\nTuesday and Wednesday...models diverge for early to mid next \nweek, with some guidance suggesting a return of elevated \nmoisture and heavy rainfall. Most guidance agrees, a cold front \nfrom the trough passing to the north arriving on Tuesday, with \nincreased moisture and the potential for heavy rainfall. \nTemperatures will also cool slightly with mostly 90s forecast \nacross the region.",
